(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with chest pressure has a first high-sensitivity troponin I of 8 ng/L and a second, drawn later, of 30 ng/L. How should the second result be interpreted?","choices":["A lab error to ignore","A normal result for a woman","A stable, reassuring result","A rising, abnormal result"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Serial troponins are drawn to watch the change. Her second high-sensitivity troponin I of 30 ng/L is above the women's 99th percentile of 12 ng/L and higher than the first, so another is ordered as cells continue to die. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has supine hypertension (high blood pressure while lying flat). Lying down she is 176/96; after 3 minutes standing she is 152/90, and she feels mildly lightheaded. Do these readings meet the orthostatic criterion?","choices":["No; readings need 10 minutes","Yes; the fall exceeds 20 mmHg","No; a fall over 30 mmHg is needed","Yes; any fall with symptoms counts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With an elevated baseline or supine hypertension the systolic fall must exceed 30 mmHg; hers is 24 and the diastolic fall is only 6. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which drug used for hypotension has only mild efficacy?","choices":["Vasopressin","Midodrine","Fludrocortisone","Droxidopa"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Droxidopa has mild efficacy, is better with supine hypertension, and is usually used as an adjunct with midodrine or fludrocortisone. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has severe congenital pulmonic stenosis with exertional dyspnea and syncope. Which procedure is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty","Tricuspid annuloplasty","Mitral balloon valvotomy","Aortic valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe pulmonic stenosis with symptoms is treated with pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty, with diuretics for right heart failure symptoms; severe congenital disease may require valve replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"An ejection fraction of 45% that has never been lower places heart failure in which group?","choices":["Preserved ejection fraction","Reduced ejection fraction","Improved ejection fraction","Mildly reduced ejection fraction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 45% sits inside the 41 to 49% band, and because it has never been lower there is no prior reduced value that would make it improved ejection fraction. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which TIMI (Thrombolysis in Myocardial Infarction) score justifies an early invasive approach?","choices":["5 or higher","3 or higher","Only the maximum","1 or higher"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A TIMI score of 3 or higher (3 to 7) calls for intensive care or emergent revascularization, the score an interventionalist uses to justify catheterization and stenting. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 71, 72"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with vasovagal syncope must have regular blood draws for a chronic illness and has fainted at two of them. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Take safety precautions during draws","Fast and restrict fluids beforehand","Take midodrine before each draw","Stop the blood draws altogether"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When a trigger cannot be avoided, safety precautions during the trigger are recommended, alongside trigger avoidance wherever possible. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl has palpitations and dizziness on standing. Within 10 minutes of standing her heart rate rises from 70 to 114, and her pressure does not fall. Does this meet the heart rate criterion for her age?","choices":["No; adolescents need a rise of 50","Yes; any rise over 20 per minute","Yes; the rise exceeds 40 per minute","No; the rise must occur in 3 minutes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In adolescents the threshold is an increase of 40 beats per minute; her rise of 44 within 10 minutes meets it. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"From what age is age itself a coronary risk factor in women?","choices":["40 years","55 years","45 years","65 years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Age counts as a nonmodifiable coronary risk factor from 55 years in women and 45 in men; premenopausal women carry less risk than men, and the gap narrows with age. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with heart failure has cool, clammy extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and sleepiness, along with orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and leg edema. Which bedside hemodynamic profile fits?","choices":["Cold and wet","Cold and dry","Warm and dry","Warm and wet"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-perfusion signs (cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure, obtundation) plus congestion signs (orthopnea, edema) place her in the cold and wet profile, hypoperfusion with congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction asks why his clinician raises one medicine at a time rather than starting everything at full strength. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Only one drug can be taken at once","Medicines stop once symptoms go","Therapy is optimized as tolerated","Full doses are unsafe in any patient"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Guideline-directed therapy is prioritized for his comorbidities and optimized as hemodynamically stable, so each class is increased as blood pressure and tolerance allow. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with orthostatic hypotension keeps standing up quickly despite being told not to, because he does not see why it matters. Which approach most improves his adherence?","choices":["Repeat the same instruction","Explain why the pressure falls","Add a vasopressor at home","Stop discussing it with him"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Understanding is the key to compliance: once a patient knows why the pressure falls on standing, slow position changes make sense and are followed. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man who received a heart transplant has exertional shortness of breath and fatigue but no chest pain. His daughter asks how he could have heart disease without pain. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Transplanted hearts can have silent ischemia","His shortness of breath rules out the heart","Transplanted hearts cannot become ischemic","Chest pain appears only with a full infarct"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A transplanted heart is one of the settings for silent ischemia, along with diabetes and peripheral neuropathy: chest discomfort may be absent, and shortness of breath or fatigue may be the only signs. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 30"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction (fraction 22%) is breathless even at rest. He does not yet take a renin-angiotensin drug. Which renin-angiotensin option fits his symptom class?","choices":["An angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor","No renin-angiotensin drug once class IV","A calcium channel blocker such as verapamil","A converting enzyme inhibitor or receptor blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms at rest are class IV. An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or receptor blocker covers class II to IV, while the angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor is recommended for class II to III. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with episodic headache, sweating, palpitations and pallor has raised plasma free metanephrines and an adrenal mass. He is scheduled for resection. What must be established before surgery?","choices":["Neither, provided the tumor is resected promptly","Alpha blockade first, then beta blockade","Either order, provided both are established before surgery","Beta blockade first, then alpha blockade"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and the order is not negotiable: isolated beta blockade leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unopposed and can precipitate a hypertensive crisis. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"When should pulmonic regurgitation be referred?","choices":["Only after a first episode of syncope","On echo or with right heart failure","Only when the cause is iatrogenic","Never, since it is always benign"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is referred when it is seen on an echocardiogram or when there are symptoms and signs of right heart failure. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with rheumatic mitral stenosis is in sinus rhythm. Which electrocardiogram finding is most expected?","choices":["Left atrial abnormality","Right atrial enlargement only","Right axis deviation alone","An expected normal tracing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis raises left atrial size and pressure, so the electrocardiogram shows left atrial abnormality. Atrial fibrillation is common in severe disease, and right ventricular hypertrophy appears with pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with hypertension and atrial fibrillation is on metoprolol. A colleague suggests adding verapamil for rate control and blood pressure. What is the concern?","choices":["Rebound hypertension on withdrawal","Severe bradycardia and heart block","Hyperkalemia and acute kidney injury","Dependent ankle edema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both agents slow the sinus and atrioventricular nodes, so combining them stacks the same effect. Both are also avoided where the ejection fraction is already reduced. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has an hour of substernal pressure. Her electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression of 2 mm in V5 and V6 without ST elevation, and her standard troponin I is 0.62 ng/mL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Infarction without ST elevation","Unstable angina","Vasospastic angina","Infarction with ST elevation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. ST-segment depression above 1 mm and/or T-wave inversion in two or more consecutive leads, with symptoms and an elevated troponin I (standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL), is a non-ST-elevation infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 86; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"With low-density lipoprotein of 190 mg/dL or more, which step is unnecessary before starting a statin?","choices":["Reviewing the full lipid profile","Ruling out hypothyroidism","Ruling out nephrotic syndrome","Calculating 10-year risk"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. At 190 mg/dL or more a high-intensity statin is begun without calculating 10-year risk; the value itself defines severe primary hypercholesterolemia. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with tricuspid stenosis has right upper quadrant pain. Examination finds a tender, enlarged liver. Which explains her pain?","choices":["Congestion of an enlarged liver","A dilated aortic root","Gallstones from carcinoid","Low cardiac output to the gut"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obstructed inflow from the right atrium to the right ventricle backs blood into the venous system, causing portal congestion; right upper quadrant pain in severe tricuspid stenosis comes from the enlarged liver. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 67, 66"},{"q":"A healthy 22-year-old man establishes care. He has never had cholesterol tested and has no symptoms or family history of heart disease. What is the most appropriate step regarding lipids?","choices":["Coronary calcium score first","No testing until he turns 40","Test only if xanthomas appear","Screen for high blood cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Screening of all adults aged 20 years or older for high blood cholesterol is recommended, whatever their symptoms; most dyslipidemia is found on laboratory testing. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which auscultatory finding is typical of mitral valve prolapse?","choices":["A blowing diastolic murmur to the apex","A diastolic rumble near the xiphoid","An opening snap after the second sound","A mid-systolic click at the apex"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse gives a mid-systolic click at the apex. It is usually benign and asymptomatic, but it may develop the symptoms and signs of mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with hypertriglyceridemia has a total cholesterol of 250 mg/dL, high-density lipoprotein of 40 mg/dL and triglyceride of 300 mg/dL. What is his non-high-density cholesterol?","choices":["290 mg/dL","50 mg/dL","190 mg/dL","210 mg/dL"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Non-high-density cholesterol is total cholesterol minus high-density cholesterol: 250 minus 40 is 210 mg/dL. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a myocardial infarction three years ago has breathlessness on exertion and orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat). Blood pressure is 104/68. There is a third heart sound (an extra sound in early diastole just after the second heart sound) and bibasilar crackles. B-type natriuretic peptide is 820. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Isolated right-sided failure from the lungs","Diastolic failure from hypertrophic disease","Systolic failure from ischemic cardiomyopathy","High-output failure from increased demand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every link of the systolic chain is here: ischemic cardiomyopathy after infarction, a low-normal blood pressure, a third heart sound from rapid filling of a dilated ventricle, and pulmonary edema, confirmed by an elevated natriuretic peptide. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with reduced ejection fraction heart failure has run low pressures for a year and asks whether his low pressure will simply go away. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["It needs a vasopressor at home","It will end once a trigger is found","Its cause may not be removable","It always resolves within weeks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic hypotension waxes and wanes, and its underlying cause, here heart failure, may be unavoidable, unlike an acute trigger that can be removed. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 44-year-old newly diagnosed hypertensive patient asks why treatment matters if she feels well. What is the strongest answer?","choices":["She will begin to feel symptoms within a few months if untreated","It causes cumulative organ damage with no reliable symptoms","Treatment is optional until organ damage is demonstrated","Feeling well means her risk is already low"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heart, brain, kidneys, aorta, arteries and retina are all injured over years, and most patients have no reliable blood pressure-related symptoms to warn them. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man on chlorthalidone presents with an acutely painful, swollen first metatarsophalangeal joint. What connects this to his medication?","choices":["Thiazide-induced angioedema","Thiazide-induced hyperkalemia","Thiazide-induced ankle edema","Hyperuricemia may precipitate gout"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, which is why uric acid is monitored alongside electrolytes and volume status in patients on a thiazide-type diuretic. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has chest pain with ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5 and V6. Which artery is the most likely culprit?","choices":["Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery","Posterior interventricular","Right marginal branch"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Leads I, aVL, V5 and V6 are the lateral leads, and lateral ST elevation points to the left circumflex artery (or a diagonal branch of the left anterior descending artery). — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with a prior heart attack and reduced ejection fraction collapsed abruptly while walking, with no warning except a brief racing heartbeat. He is pale, his pressure is 86/52 and his heart rate is 150 and regular. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension","Cardiogenic syncope from an arrhythmia","Situational reflex syncope","Vasovagal reflex syncope"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Abrupt collapse with minimal prodrome after palpitations, in a patient with coronary disease and reduced ejection fraction, with a rapid regular rhythm, points to an arrhythmic cardiac cause. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with an inferior ST-elevation myocardial infarction suddenly develops complete heart block on the monitor. Which artery is most likely occluded?","choices":["Anterior interventricular artery","Diagonal artery","Right coronary artery","Left circumflex artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The right coronary artery supplies the atrioventricular node, so an occlusion that produces complete heart block points to it. That is why right coronary artery infarcts are the ones most likely to cause dysrhythmias. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has an ST-elevation myocardial infarction at a hospital three hours from any intervention unit. She had a hemorrhagic stroke five months ago. Which therapy is absolutely contraindicated?","choices":["Transfer for intervention","Chewable aspirin","Thrombolytic therapy","A beta blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A hemorrhagic stroke in the previous year is an absolute contraindication to thrombolytics, as are active bleeding, pregnancy, major surgery or trauma within four weeks and uncontrolled hypertension above 190/100. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 67, 75"},{"q":"A 55-year-old South Asian man without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 150 mg/dL and a calculated 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 12%. His father had a heart attack at 50. After discussion he favors treatment. What is the most appropriate therapy?","choices":["Moderate-intensity statin","A fibrate for his cholesterol","High-intensity statin with ezetimibe","Lifestyle alone without any drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At intermediate risk with risk enhancers (South Asian ancestry, premature family history) and a discussion that favors therapy, start a moderate-intensity statin to lower low-density lipoprotein by 30% or more. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which setting is typical of micturition syncope?","choices":["Exertion while climbing stairs","Voiding while standing at night","Shock at hearing bad news","Diarrhea for several days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nocturnal urination after abruptly standing from bed, voiding in the standing position, triggers an exaggerated autonomic reflex with vagal activity and reduced vascular tone. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has a white-gray ring around the edge of each cornea (corneal arcus). His low-density lipoprotein is 250 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride, and his father died of a heart attack at 52. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Corneal arcus before 40 usually indicates a genetic condition, and low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 with normal triglyceride and premature family coronary disease fits the heterozygous form, in which arcus is common. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman consistently reads 150/92 in clinic but her seven-day home average is 118/74 with a validated upper-arm device and correct technique. Which pattern does this represent?","choices":["Resistant hypertension","Stage 2 hypertension","White coat hypertension","Masked hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: raised office readings with normal out-of-office readings. Recognizing it avoids treating a patient whose true pressure is normal. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man is newly diagnosed with heart failure and will start a loop diuretic. Which laboratory panel should be checked before starting?","choices":["Hepatic function panel","Coagulation panel","Complete metabolic panel","Thyroid function panel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The metabolic panel gives potassium and renal function (blood urea nitrogen, creatinine and filtration rate), both of which a potassium-wasting loop diuretic affects. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with two prior heart attacks, diabetes and hypertension takes a maximal statin. Her low-density lipoprotein is 95 mg/dL, and she takes no other lipid drug. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add evolocumab","Add ezetimibe","Stop the statin","Add niacin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In very high-risk disease, when low-density lipoprotein stays 70 or more on a maximally tolerated statin, adding ezetimibe is reasonable, and it comes before any injectable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a mechanical aortic valve on warfarin is scheduled for a colectomy that requires stopping it. Which plan should be avoided?","choices":["Stopping warfarin with no bridge","Bridging with heparin","Resuming a vitamin K antagonist","Rechecking his ratio after surgery"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If surgery requires interrupting warfarin, it must be bridged with heparin until the international normalized ratio is therapeutic; leaving a mechanical valve uncovered is the thing to avoid. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman recovering from a myocardial infarction cannot take a beta blocker. Her echocardiogram shows preserved left ventricular function. Which agent can reduce her mortality?","choices":["Warfarin","Diltiazem","Amlodipine","Nitroglycerin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcium channel blockers do not improve prognosis overall after infarction, but diltiazem and verapamil reduce mortality in patients with preserved left ventricular function, which she has. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 78-year-old woman with a prior ischemic stroke has taken a high-intensity statin for years without problems. She asks whether she should stop it now that she is over 75. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Switch to a fibrate instead","Only lifestyle is advised now","Continuing it is reasonable","Statins are stopped at 75"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Above 75 with clinical atherosclerotic disease, continuing a high-intensity statin is reasonable, as is starting a moderate- or high-intensity statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"How are most patients with dyslipidemia identified?","choices":["From chest pain that prompts testing","From attacks of acute pancreatitis","From abnormal lipid laboratory values","From xanthomas found on the skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most patients have no specific signs or symptoms, and the majority are identified after lipid abnormalities are detected in laboratory values. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which drug class is recommended to prevent heart failure in patients with diabetes who have no structural heart disease?","choices":["Beta blocker started at diagnosis","Hydralazine with isosorbide dinitrate","Sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor","Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prevention in stage A, risk factors with a normal heart, carries one strong drug recommendation: a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor for patients with diabetes, ahead of any beta blocker, which is a stage B therapy. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. She stands up quickly from squatting while the examiner listens. How does the murmur change?","choices":["It becomes softer","It gains a click","It becomes louder","It does not change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The mitral regurgitation murmur decreases with standing or the Valsalva maneuver and increases with squatting and handgrip, which raise resistance to ventricular ejection. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the commonest cause to exclude before labeling hypertension as resistant?","choices":["Medication nonadherence","Excess alcohol intake","Incorrect cuff size","Undiagnosed primary aldosteronism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and it heads a list that also includes measurement error, white coat effect, high sodium or alcohol intake, interfering drugs, and undiagnosed sleep apnea or secondary hypertension. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has sudden severe breathlessness, crackles and edema. Her B-type natriuretic peptide is 1,150. How should this level be read?","choices":["The result is indeterminate","Only chronic heart failure fits","Acute heart failure is ruled out","Acute heart failure is likely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Above 900 acute heart failure is likely. Above 400 heart failure is likely in general, 100 to 400 is indeterminate, and below 100 rules acute failure out. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with aortic stenosis on echocardiogram plays competitive tennis and often skips fluids on hot days. She asks what she should change. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Restrict fluids to keep her volume low","Increase training to strengthen her heart","No changes until symptoms appear","Avoid competitive sports and dehydration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic stenosis makes the patient preload dependent, so she should avoid strenuous physical activity and competitive sports and avoid dehydration and hypovolemia, both of which reduce the preload she relies on. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 12-year-old boy with confirmed homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia has total cholesterol of 610 mg/dL on a high-intensity statin with ezetimibe. What is the treatment of choice for his disorder?","choices":["Low-density lipoprotein apheresis","Add a fibrate for his triglyceride","Switch to a moderate-intensity statin","Very low-fat diet alone for a year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Apheresis is the treatment of choice, like dialysis: the elective removal of low-density particles from the circulation. Most patients also need several drugs, starting with high-intensity statins. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has a Corrigan pulse (a very rapid upstroke followed by rapid collapse) and a de Musset sign (head bobbing with each heartbeat). Which valve lesion is most likely?","choices":["Aortic stenosis","Aortic regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The Corrigan pulse, also called a water hammer or collapsing pulse, and the de Musset sign are findings of chronic aortic regurgitation, along with a diastolic blowing murmur and a displaced point of maximal impulse. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"How does hypertension prevalence change with age?","choices":["It rises sharply","It falls after middle age","It peaks in early adulthood","It remains flat across adult life"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, which is why advancing age sits among the major risk factors and why a young patient with hypertension raises more suspicion of a secondary cause than an older one does. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man is found to have a raised creatinine. What is required before chronic kidney disease can be diagnosed?","choices":["Albuminuria alone, regardless of duration","Structural renal disease demonstrated on imaging","A kidney abnormality persisting three months","A single raised creatinine measurement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A single raised value may reflect a reversible insult, so duration is part of the definition rather than an optional confirmation. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 43-year-old man has a fasting triglyceride of 620 mg/dL. He drinks heavily most nights. Before any drug is started, which additional conditions should be evaluated?","choices":["Diabetes, kidney disease, hypothyroidism","Anorexia nervosa and biliary cirrhosis","Lipoprotein(a) and coronary calcium","Tendon xanthomas and corneal arcus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. With triglyceride of 500 or more rule out diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, alcoholism, pregnancy and hypothyroidism; his alcohol history is important too. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man hospitalized with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction asks why a defibrillator is being discussed. Which cause accounts for about half of cardiac arrests in hospitalized patients like him?","choices":["Acute pulmonary embolism","Complete heart block","Low potassium alone","Ventricular arrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 52% of cardiac arrests in hospitalized patients with reduced ejection fraction are due to ventricular arrhythmias, which is the reason for defibrillator protection in those who meet the criteria. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with high-output heart failure from untreated hyperthyroidism has leg edema and breathlessness. Which treatment approach is most appropriate?","choices":["Start verapamil to slow the heart rate","Treat the thyroid disease and add a diuretic","Refer for heart transplant evaluation","Start dobutamine to support contractility"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High-output failure is treated by correcting the underlying cause of increased demand, here hyperthyroidism, with a diuretic for congestion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has chest pressure when he climbs two flights of stairs. It fades after three minutes of rest and has followed the same pattern for months. His resting electrocardiogram is normal. Which test is most appropriate next?","choices":["Ergonovine provocation test","Exercise treadmill stress test","Posterior-lead electrocardiogram","Serial troponin I measurements"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable angina is diagnosed by stress-test-induced electrocardiogram changes, usually T-wave inversion, on an exercise (treadmill), nuclear or cardiac computed tomography stress test. Coronary angiography remains the gold standard. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 38"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man had a large blood loss after surgery 2 days ago and now feels faint on standing. His pressure falls 30 mmHg with a heart rate rise from 72 to 100. Which is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Start midodrine for neurogenic syncope","Start fludrocortisone for fluid","Order a tilt-table test","Treat the underlying blood loss"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemorrhage is an example cause of non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension, and the principle is to treat the underlying condition, the real diagnosis. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with long-standing pulmonary hypertension and a dilated right ventricle develops a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which process most likely produced her valve leak?","choices":["Endocarditis of the leaflets","A prior valvuloplasty","Dilation of the tricuspid annulus","Rheumatic leaflet narrowing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most common cause of tricuspid regurgitation is dilation of the tricuspid annulus from right ventricular dilation in the setting of pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman is referred with hypertension diagnosed at 32 and an adrenal nodule found incidentally on imaging. What is the underlying abnormality if aldosteronism is confirmed?","choices":["Glucocorticoid excess promoting sodium retention","Excess renin driving aldosterone production","Catecholamine excess from an adrenal medullary tumor","Autonomous aldosterone production independent of renin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The adrenal gland secretes aldosterone without the usual renin-angiotensin signal, so sodium is retained and potassium wasted regardless of volume status. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 54-year-old arrives late for a routine visit, is taken straight to the room and has her blood pressure measured immediately while she describes her morning. The cuff is placed over her sleeve and her legs are crossed. The reading is 152/94. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat it after five minutes of quiet rest","Diagnose stage 2 hypertension and start two agents","Reassure her that a single elevated reading is meaningless","Order plasma metanephrines to exclude pheochromocytoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every departure here inflates the reading: no rest period, talking during the measurement, a sleeved arm and crossed legs. A diagnosis rests on readings taken to standard, and acting on this one would start treatment on an artifact. Correct technique matters as much as the rest period. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man had his diuretic dose increased 10 days ago. He now has lightheadedness on standing, a 28 mmHg systolic fall and a heart rate rise from 70 to 94. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Start fludrocortisone","Add droxidopa as an adjunct","Start midodrine for the pressure","Reduce the recently increased diuretic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The offending agent is removed first: iatrogenic hypotension is treated by reducing or removing the medication, with slow position changes meanwhile. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 23"}]};
